<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horemheb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1319–1292 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Horemhab, Haremheb, Haremhab · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horemheb was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 18th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling for at least 14 years between 1319 and 1292 BCE. Believed to have been of common birth, he rose to be commander-in-chief of the army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> and Ay, and was linked to the royal family only by marriage to Mutnedjmet, thought, though this is disputed, to be Ay's daughter. As king he restabilised Egypt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-period">Amarna Period</a>: he demolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s monuments, usurped those of Tutankhamun and Ay, and issued the Edict of Horemheb against corruption. Having no surviving son, he named his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Paramessu as his successor, who reigned as Ramesses I. His tomb, KV57, was discovered in 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horemheb.jpg" alt="Horemheb" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Captmondo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StatueOfHoremhebAndTheGodHorus-DetailOfHoremheb01_KunsthistorischesMuseum_Nov13-10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as Egypt declined: the Akkar Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of Suppiluliuma I of Hatti, and Tutankhamun and his general <b>Horemheb</b> scrambled to keep Egypt's hold on the southern Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kingship passed by birth, usually to the king’s son, but when a dynasty failed the throne could go to the men who served it: Ay and the general <b>Horemheb</b> rose from the royal court, and Horemheb’s vizier became king as Ramesses I in 1292 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herakleopolis-magna">Herakleopolis Magna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.08,30.93&amp;year=-1306" aria-label="Herakleopolis Magna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5357 1100 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Horemheb"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5807 1370h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5807 1370h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.08,30.93&amp;year=-1306">What was here in 1306 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
